They overlap heavily. The practical differences:

```typescript
// interface: extendable, supports declaration merging
interface User { id: number; name: string }
interface Admin extends User { role: "admin" }
interface User { email?: string }          // merges into the first User

// type: can name unions, tuples, primitives, mapped/conditional types
type ID = string | number;
type Pair = [string, number];
type Admin2 = User & { role: "admin" };
type Readonly<T> = { readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] };
```

Guideline: use `interface` for object shapes that others may extend (especially public APIs), and `type` for unions, intersections, and anything computed.

