Subscribers register for a specific event class, so `publish` delivers only matching events and the compiler checks the types.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;
import java.util.function.Consumer;

public final class EventBus {
    private final Map<Class<?>, List<Consumer<?>>> handlers = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public <E> Runnable subscribe(Class<E> type, Consumer<? super E> handler) {
        var list = handlers.computeIfAbsent(type, k -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>());
        list.add(handler);
        return () -> list.remove(handler);           // returns an unsubscribe action
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <E> void publish(E event) {
        // Walk the class hierarchy so subscribers to a supertype also receive subclasses.
        for (Class<?> c = event.getClass(); c != null; c = c.getSuperclass()) {
            for (Consumer<?> h : handlers.getOrDefault(c, List.of()))
                ((Consumer<E>) h).accept(event);
        }
    }

    // ---- demo ----
    record UserCreated(String name) {}
    record OrderPlaced(int id) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var bus = new EventBus();
        Runnable unsub = bus.subscribe(UserCreated.class, e -> System.out.println("welcome " + e.name()));
        bus.subscribe(OrderPlaced.class, e -> System.out.println("order " + e.id()));

        bus.publish(new UserCreated("Ana"));
        bus.publish(new OrderPlaced(7));
        unsub.run();
        bus.publish(new UserCreated("Omar"));       // no output: unsubscribed
    }
}
```

Note that `ConcurrentHashMap` is missing from the imports above. Add `import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;`. `CopyOnWriteArrayList` makes iteration safe while handlers subscribe or unsubscribe during a publish. It's a good fit when reads far outnumber writes.

Records give you immutability for free. The builder collects values, validates them once in one place, and produces the record.

```java
import java.util.Objects;

public record HttpRequest(String method, String url, int timeoutMs, String body) {

    // Compact constructor: runs on every construction path (builder or direct call)
    public HttpRequest {
        Objects.requireNonNull(method, "method");
        Objects.requireNonNull(url, "url");
        if (!url.startsWith("https://")) throw new IllegalArgumentException("https only: " + url);
        if (timeoutMs <= 0 || timeoutMs > 60_000) throw new IllegalArgumentException("timeout out of range");
        method = method.toUpperCase();
    }

    public static Builder builder(String url) { return new Builder(url); }

    public static final class Builder {
        private final String url;
        private String method = "GET";
        private int timeoutMs = 5_000;
        private String body;

        private Builder(String url) { this.url = url; }

        public Builder method(String m)    { this.method = m; return this; }
        public Builder timeout(int ms)     { this.timeoutMs = ms; return this; }
        public Builder body(String b)      { this.body = b; return this; }

        public HttpRequest build() { return new HttpRequest(method, url, timeoutMs, body); }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var req = HttpRequest.builder("https://api.example.com/items")
                             .method("post")
                             .timeout(2_000)
                             .body("{\"a\":1}")
                             .build();
        System.out.println(req);
    }
}
```

The compact constructor is the single place where invariants are checked, so no path can create an invalid object. Because the record is immutable, you can share it across threads without locks.

Define constraints with annotations, then check any object with reflection. This is the same mechanism behind Bean Validation (`@NotNull`, `@Size`).

```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;
import java.lang.reflect.Field;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Validation {

    @Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
    @Target(ElementType.FIELD)
    public @interface NotBlank {
        String message() default "must not be blank";
    }

    @Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
    @Target(ElementType.FIELD)
    public @interface Range {
        int min();
        int max();
    }

    public static List<String> validate(Object obj) throws IllegalAccessException {
        var errors = new ArrayList<String>();
        for (Field f : obj.getClass().getDeclaredFields()) {
            f.setAccessible(true);
            Object value = f.get(obj);

            if (f.isAnnotationPresent(NotBlank.class)) {
                if (value == null || value.toString().isBlank())
                    errors.add(f.getName() + " " + f.getAnnotation(NotBlank.class).message());
            }
            if (f.isAnnotationPresent(Range.class) && value instanceof Integer i) {
                Range r = f.getAnnotation(Range.class);
                if (i < r.min() || i > r.max())
                    errors.add(f.getName() + " must be between " + r.min() + " and " + r.max());
            }
        }
        return errors;
    }

    static class Signup {
        @NotBlank String username = "  ";
        @Range(min = 13, max = 120) int age = 9;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        validate(new Signup()).forEach(System.out::println);
        // username must not be blank
        // age must be between 13 and 120
    }
}
```

Use `getAnnotation` only on fields where `isAnnotationPresent` is true. Remember that `RUNTIME` retention is required, or the annotation is invisible to reflection. Production code should cache the reflected field list per class, because reflection is slow in hot paths.

```java
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Aggregator {
    static final ExecutorService POOL = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor();

    static String fetchPrice(String vendor) {
        try { Thread.sleep(vendor.length() * 300L); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
        return vendor + ":42";
    }

    static CompletableFuture<String> priceFrom(String vendor) {
        return CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> fetchPrice(vendor), POOL)
            .completeOnTimeout(vendor + ":unavailable", 800, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)  // slow vendor -> placeholder
            .exceptionally(ex -> vendor + ":error");                               // failure -> placeholder
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<CompletableFuture<String>> futures = List.of("acme", "globex", "initech")
            .stream().map(Aggregator::priceFrom).toList();

        String result = CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(CompletableFuture[]::new))
            .thenApply(v -> futures.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).collect(Collectors.joining(", ")))
            .orTimeout(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
            .join();

        System.out.println(result);
        POOL.shutdown();
    }
}
```

`completeOnTimeout` supplies a value after a delay and keeps the pipeline moving. `exceptionally` converts failures to fallbacks. Use `orTimeout` on the combined future to cap the total wait. Avoid calling `join()` inside another async task that is waiting on the same pool, because that can deadlock.

No frameworks or dependencies. `com.sun.net.httpserver` ships with the JDK.

```java
import com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpExchange;
import com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpServer;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.OutputStream;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class MiniServer {
    static final Map<String, String> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        HttpServer server = HttpServer.create(new InetSocketAddress(8080), 0);

        server.createContext("/kv", exchange -> {
            try {
                String key = exchange.getRequestURI().getQuery();   // /kv?key=name
                switch (exchange.getRequestMethod()) {
                    case "GET" -> {
                        String v = store.get(key);
                        if (v == null) send(exchange, 404, "{\"error\":\"not found\"}");
                        else send(exchange, 200, "{\"value\":\"" + v + "\"}");
                    }
                    case "PUT" -> {
                        String body = new String(exchange.getRequestBody().readAllBytes(), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
                        store.put(key, body.strip());
                        send(exchange, 204, "");
                    }
                    case "DELETE" -> {
                        store.remove(key);
                        send(exchange, 204, "");
                    }
                    default -> send(exchange, 405, "{\"error\":\"method not allowed\"}");
                }
            } finally {
                exchange.close();
            }
        });

        server.setExecutor(Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor());
        server.start();
        System.out.println("Listening on http://localhost:8080/kv");
    }

    static void send(HttpExchange ex, int status, String body) throws IOException {
        byte[] bytes = body.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
        ex.getResponseHeaders().add("Content-Type", "application/json");
        ex.sendResponseHeaders(status, bytes.length == 0 ? -1 : bytes.length);
        if (bytes.length > 0) try (OutputStream os = ex.getResponseBody()) { os.write(bytes); }
    }
}
```

Test with: `curl -X PUT -d "hello" "localhost:8080/kv?key=greet"` and then `curl "localhost:8080/kv?key=greet"`. Note that `send` passes `-1` for no-body responses, which tells the server to send no content. Parsing query strings properly would need a real parser, and the JSON here is built by hand, so a library such as Jackson is safer when values may contain quotes.

```java
import java.sql.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Accounts {
    private final String url;   // e.g. jdbc:postgresql://localhost/bank

    public Accounts(String url) { this.url = url; }

    /** Moves money atomically: both updates commit together or neither does. */
    public void transfer(long fromId, long toId, BigDecimal amount) throws SQLException {
        try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, "app", System.getenv("DB_PASS"))) {
            conn.setAutoCommit(false);
            try {
                debit(conn, fromId, amount);
                credit(conn, toId, amount);
                conn.commit();
            } catch (SQLException | RuntimeException e) {
                conn.rollback();
                throw e;
            }
        }
    }

    private void debit(Connection c, long id, BigDecimal amount) throws SQLException {
        try (PreparedStatement ps = c.prepareStatement(
                "UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ? AND balance >= ?")) {
            ps.setBigDecimal(1, amount);
            ps.setLong(2, id);
            ps.setBigDecimal(3, amount);
            if (ps.executeUpdate() != 1)
                throw new IllegalStateException("insufficient funds or unknown account " + id);
        }
    }

    private void credit(Connection c, long id, BigDecimal amount) throws SQLException {
        try (PreparedStatement ps = c.prepareStatement(
                "UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + ? WHERE id = ?")) {
            ps.setBigDecimal(1, amount);
            ps.setLong(2, id);
            if (ps.executeUpdate() != 1)
                throw new IllegalStateException("unknown account " + id);
        }
    }
}
```

Three details matter here. `BigDecimal` avoids the rounding errors of `double` for money. The balance check sits inside the `WHERE` clause, so it can't race with other transactions. Any failure triggers `rollback`. In production, use a connection pool such as HikariCP instead of `DriverManager.getConnection` on every call.

Parses arithmetic like `2 + 3 * (4 - 1)` into an AST, then evaluates it. Respects operator precedence and parentheses.

```java
import java.util.List;

public class Calc {
    sealed interface Expr permits Num, Bin, Neg {}
    record Num(double value) implements Expr {}
    record Bin(char op, Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {}
    record Neg(Expr inner) implements Expr {}

    static double eval(Expr e) {
        return switch (e) {
            case Num n -> n.value();
            case Neg n -> -eval(n.inner());
            case Bin b -> {
                double l = eval(b.left()), r = eval(b.right());
                yield switch (b.op()) {
                    case '+' -> l + r;
                    case '-' -> l - r;
                    case '*' -> l * r;
                    case '/' -> {
                        if (r == 0) throw new ArithmeticException("division by zero");
                        yield l / r;
                    }
                    default -> throw new IllegalStateException("op " + b.op());
                };
            }
        };
    }

    // ---- parser: expr -> term (('+'|'-') term)* ; term -> factor (('*'|'/') factor)* ; factor -> '-' factor | num | '(' expr ')'
    static class Parser {
        private final String src;
        private int pos = 0;
        Parser(String src) { this.src = src.replace(" ", ""); }

        Expr parse() {
            Expr e = expr();
            if (pos != src.length()) throw error("unexpected '" + src.charAt(pos) + "'");
            return e;
        }

        private Expr expr() {
            Expr left = term();
            while (peek('+') || peek('-')) {
                char op = src.charAt(pos++);
                left = new Bin(op, left, term());
            }
            return left;
        }

        private Expr term() {
            Expr left = factor();
            while (peek('*') || peek('/')) {
                char op = src.charAt(pos++);
                left = new Bin(op, left, factor());
            }
            return left;
        }

        private Expr factor() {
            if (peek('-')) { pos++; return new Neg(factor()); }
            if (peek('(')) {
                pos++;
                Expr e = expr();
                if (!peek(')')) throw error("expected ')'");
                pos++;
                return e;
            }
            int start = pos;
            while (pos < src.length() && (Character.isDigit(src.charAt(pos)) || src.charAt(pos) == '.')) pos++;
            if (start == pos) throw error("expected number");
            return new Num(Double.parseDouble(src.substring(start, pos)));
        }

        private boolean peek(char c) { return pos < src.length() && src.charAt(pos) == c; }
        private IllegalArgumentException error(String msg) {
            return new IllegalArgumentException(msg + " at position " + pos);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (String s : List.of("2 + 3 * (4 - 1)", "-(2+3)*2", "10 / (5 - 5)", "2 +")) {
            try {
                System.out.println(s + " = " + eval(new Parser(s).parse()));
            } catch (RuntimeException ex) {
                System.out.println(s + " -> " + ex.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Each grammar rule becomes one method, so precedence comes from call depth: `term` calls `factor`, so `*` binds tighter than `+`. The sealed hierarchy lets the `switch` in `eval` be exhaustive with no default case for the node types.

`computeIfAbsent` runs the expensive function at most once per key, even when many threads ask for the same key at the same time.

```java
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.function.Function;

public final class Memoizer {
    public static <K, V> Function<K, V> memoize(Function<K, V> fn) {
        Map<K, V> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        return key -> cache.computeIfAbsent(key, fn);
    }

    // Caches futures so that concurrent callers wait on one computation
    public static <K, V> Function<K, V> memoizeAsync(Function<K, V> fn) {
        Map<K, CompletableFuture<V>> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        return key -> cache.computeIfAbsent(key,
                k -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fn.apply(k))).join();
    }

    static long slowSquare(long n) {
        try { Thread.sleep(500); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
        return n * n;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        var fast = memoize(Memoizer::slowSquare);
        long t = System.nanoTime();
        System.out.println(fast.apply(12));   // ~500 ms
        System.out.println(fast.apply(12));   // instant
        System.out.printf("%d ms%n", (System.nanoTime() - t) / 1_000_000);
    }
}
```

Caution: the mapping function given to `computeIfAbsent` must be short and must not modify the same map, or it can deadlock or throw `IllegalStateException`. That is why the async variant stores the future, which is created quickly, and the slow work runs outside the map's lock. Also note that the cache has no size limit, so use it only for bounded key sets.

Streams a large file line by line, so memory use stays flat no matter the file size.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class WordCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        Path path = Path.of(args.length > 0 ? args[0] : "book.txt");

        try (var lines = Files.lines(path, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {   // closes the file
            Map<String, Long> counts = lines
                .flatMap(line -> java.util.Arrays.stream(line.toLowerCase().split("\\W+")))
                .filter(w -> !w.isBlank())
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(w -> w, Collectors.counting()));

            counts.entrySet().stream()
                .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Long>comparingByValue().reversed()
                        .thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()))
                .limit(10)
                .forEach(e -> System.out.printf("%-15s %d%n", e.getKey(), e.getValue()));
        }
    }
}
```

`Files.lines` must be closed, which is why it sits in try-with-resources. Note that `groupingBy` still keeps every distinct word in memory, so for a truly huge vocabulary use a count-min sketch or write partial counts to disk.

Shows the classic monitor pattern that underlies `java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue`. It's worth understanding even if you use the library version.

```java
public class BoundedBuffer<T> {
    private final Object[] items;
    private int head, tail, count;

    public BoundedBuffer(int capacity) {
        items = new Object[capacity];
    }

    public synchronized void put(T item) throws InterruptedException {
        while (count == items.length)      // loop, not if: guards against spurious wakeups
            wait();
        items[tail] = item;
        tail = (tail + 1) % items.length;
        count++;
        notifyAll();
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {
        while (count == 0)
            wait();
        T item = (T) items[head];
        items[head] = null;                // let GC reclaim it
        head = (head + 1) % items.length;
        count--;
        notifyAll();
        return item;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var buf = new BoundedBuffer<Integer>(3);

        Thread producer = new Thread(() -> {
            try { for (int i = 1; i <= 6; i++) { buf.put(i); System.out.println("put " + i); } }
            catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
        });
        Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {
            try { for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) System.out.println("took " + buf.take()); }
            catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
        });

        producer.start(); consumer.start();
    }
}
```

Use `while` around `wait()` every time, because a thread can wake up without a signal. `notifyAll` is safer than `notify` because it wakes both producers and consumers. In real systems, prefer `ArrayBlockingQueue` or `LinkedBlockingQueue`.

A `Collector` is built from four parts: a supplier, an accumulator, a combiner, and a finisher. Implementing one shows how `Collectors.toList` and friends actually work.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class Collectors2 {
    // Joins the values of each group into one comma-separated string
    public static <T, K> Collector<T, ?, Map<K, String>> joiningBy(
            Function<T, K> key, Function<T, String> value) {

        Supplier<Map<K, StringJoiner>> supplier = HashMap::new;

        BiConsumer<Map<K, StringJoiner>, T> accumulator = (m, t) ->
            m.computeIfAbsent(key.apply(t), k -> new StringJoiner(", ")).add(value.apply(t));

        BinaryOperator<Map<K, StringJoiner>> combiner = (a, b) -> {     // used only by parallel streams
            b.forEach((k, sj) -> a.merge(k, sj, (x, y) -> { x.merge(y); return x; }));
            return a;
        };

        Function<Map<K, StringJoiner>, Map<K, String>> finisher = m -> {
            Map<K, String> out = new TreeMap<>();
            m.forEach((k, sj) -> out.put(k, sj.toString()));
            return out;
        };

        return Collector.of(supplier, accumulator, combiner, finisher);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        record Pet(String owner, String name) {}
        var pets = List.of(new Pet("Ana", "Rex"), new Pet("Omar", "Tom"), new Pet("Ana", "Mia"));

        Map<String, String> result = pets.stream()
            .collect(joiningBy(Pet::owner, Pet::name));
        System.out.println(result);   // {Ana=Rex, Mia, Omar=Tom}
    }
}
```

The combiner runs only for parallel streams, and it's easy to get wrong. Test with `.parallel()` when you write one. Note that `StringJoiner.merge` appends another joiner's contents, which is why the combiner calls it.

`ServiceLoader` discovers implementations listed in `META-INF/services`, so new plugins can be added as separate JARs without changing the host app.

Host application:
```java
import java.util.ServiceLoader;

public interface Plugin {
    String name();
    String run(String input);
}

public class Host {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (Plugin p : ServiceLoader.load(Plugin.class)) {
            System.out.println(p.name() + " -> " + p.run("hello"));
        }
    }
}
```

A plugin JAR contains:
```java
public class Shouter implements Plugin {
    public String name() { return "shouter"; }
    public String run(String input) { return input.toUpperCase() + "!"; }
}
```

And the file `META-INF/services/com.example.Plugin` contains one line:
```
com.example.Shouter
```

Run with `java -cp host.jar:shouter.jar com.example.Host`. Each plugin should fail in isolation, so wrap calls like this:
```java
try {
    System.out.println(p.run("hello"));
} catch (ServiceConfigurationError | RuntimeException e) {
    System.err.println("plugin " + p.getClass().getName() + " failed: " + e);
}
```

For stronger isolation, load plugins in a separate `URLClassLoader` and use the module system (`module-info.java` with `provides ... with`) on Java 9+.

Replaces `{{name}}` placeholders and escapes values by default, which prevents HTML injection. Useful for small generated pages.

```java
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public final class Template {
    private static final Pattern VAR = Pattern.compile("\\{\\{\\s*(\\w+)\\s*}}");

    public static String render(String template, Map<String, Object> vars) {
        Matcher m = VAR.matcher(template);
        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();
        while (m.find()) {
            Object value = vars.getOrDefault(m.group(1), "");
            m.appendReplacement(out, Matcher.quoteReplacement(escape(String.valueOf(value))));
        }
        m.appendTail(out);
        return out.toString();
    }

    static String escape(String s) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(s.length());
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            switch (c) {
                case '&'  -> sb.append("&amp;");
                case '<'  -> sb.append("&lt;");
                case '>'  -> sb.append("&gt;");
                case '"'  -> sb.append("&quot;");
                case '\'' -> sb.append("&#x27;");
                default   -> sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String page = "<h1>Hello {{name}}</h1><p>{{bio}}</p>";
        System.out.println(render(page, Map.of(
            "name", "Ana",
            "bio", "<script>alert(1)</script>")));
        // <h1>Hello Ana</h1><p>&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;</p>
    }
}
```

`appendReplacement` treats `$` and `\` specially, so `Matcher.quoteReplacement` is required for untrusted values. This engine doesn't handle loops or conditionals. For those, use a real engine such as Thymeleaf or Freemarker.

Swing UI code must run on the Event Dispatch Thread (EDT). `SwingWorker` runs slow work in the background and publishes results back to the EDT.

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.util.List;

public class Downloader extends JFrame {
    private final JProgressBar bar = new JProgressBar(0, 100);
    private final JButton start = new JButton("Start");
    private final JTextArea log = new JTextArea(10, 40);

    public Downloader() {
        super("Background task");
        log.setEditable(false);
        start.addActionListener(e -> run());
        setLayout(new BorderLayout(8, 8));
        add(start, BorderLayout.NORTH);
        add(new JScrollPane(log), BorderLayout.CENTER);
        add(bar, BorderLayout.SOUTH);
        setDefaultCloseOperation(EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        pack();
        setLocationRelativeTo(null);
    }

    private void run() {
        start.setEnabled(false);
        SwingWorker<Void, String> worker = new SwingWorker<>() {
            @Override
            protected Void doInBackground() throws Exception {  // NOT on the EDT
                for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
                    Thread.sleep(300);                          // pretend to download
                    publish("chunk " + i);                      // delivered to process()
                    setProgress(i * 10);                        // updates the progress property
                }
                return null;
            }

            @Override
            protected void process(List<String> chunks) {      // on the EDT
                chunks.forEach(c -> log.append(c + "\n"));
            }

            @Override
            protected void done() {                            // on the EDT
                start.setEnabled(true);
                try { get(); log.append("finished\n"); }
                catch (Exception ex) { log.append("failed: " + ex.getMessage() + "\n"); }
            }
        };
        worker.addPropertyChangeListener(evt -> {
            if ("progress".equals(evt.getPropertyName())) bar.setValue((Integer) evt.getNewValue());
        });
        worker.execute();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() -> new Downloader().setVisible(true));
    }
}
```

Note the anonymous `SwingWorker<Void, String>`: the second type parameter is what `publish` sends to `process`. Keep the worker's `execute()` call after attaching listeners, so the first events are not missed.

The Java Memory Model (JMM) defines when one thread's writes become visible to another. Without synchronization, the compiler and CPU may reorder operations, so a thread can see stale or partly built objects.

```java
class Broken {
    static Config config;                          // no synchronization
    static void init() { config = new Config(); }  // another thread may see a half-built object
}

class Fixed {
    static volatile boolean ready = false;   // volatile write happens-before any later read of it
    static int payload;

    static void producer() {
        payload = 42;          // ordinary write...
        ready = true;          // ...is visible to any thread that reads ready == true
    }

    static void consumer() {
        while (!ready) Thread.onSpinWait();
        System.out.println(payload);   // guaranteed to print 42
    }
}
```

Key rules:
- A `synchronized` block's unlock happens-before the next lock on the same monitor.
- A `volatile` write happens-before every later read of that variable.
- `Thread.start()` and `Thread.join()` create happens-before edges.
- `final` fields are safely published once the constructor finishes, which is why immutable objects are thread-safe without locks.

`volatile` gives visibility and ordering, but not atomicity. `count++` on a volatile is still a race, so use `AtomicInteger`. For shared data structures, prefer `java.util.concurrent` classes over hand-rolled locks.

```html
<input id="filter" placeholder="Filter by name…">
<table id="t">
  <thead><tr>
    <th data-key="name">Name</th>
    <th data-key="city">City</th>
    <th data-key="score">Score</th>
  </tr></thead>
  <tbody></tbody>
</table>
<nav>
  <button id="prev">Prev</button>
  <span id="page"></span>
  <button id="next">Next</button>
</nav>

<style>
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: system-ui; }
  th, td { padding: .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; text-align: left; }
  th { cursor: pointer; user-select: none; }
  th[aria-sort="ascending"]::after { content: " ▲"; }
  th[aria-sort="descending"]::after { content: " ▼"; }
  @media (max-width: 500px) { th, td { font-size: 14px; } }
</style>

<script>
const rows = Array.from({ length: 73 }, (_, i) => ({
  name: ["Ana", "Omar", "Lina", "Sam"][i % 4] + " " + i,
  city: ["Cairo", "Amman", "Nablus"][i % 3],
  score: (i * 37) % 100,
}));

const PAGE = 10;
let sortKey = null, asc = true, page = 0, query = "";

const tbody = document.querySelector("tbody");
const pageLabel = document.getElementById("page");

function view() {
  let data = rows.filter(r => r.name.toLowerCase().includes(query));
  if (sortKey) data = data.sort((a, b) =>
    (a[sortKey] > b[sortKey] ? 1 : a[sortKey] < b[sortKey] ? -1 : 0) * (asc ? 1 : -1));
  return data;
}

function render() {
  const data = view();
  const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(data.length / PAGE));
  page = Math.min(page, pages - 1);
  tbody.replaceChildren(...data.slice(page * PAGE, page * PAGE + PAGE).map(r => {
    const tr = document.createElement("tr");
    for (const k of ["name", "city", "score"]) {
      const td = document.createElement("td");
      td.textContent = r[k];        // textContent: no HTML injection from data
      tr.append(td);
    }
    return tr;
  }));
  pageLabel.textContent = `${page + 1} / ${pages}`;
  document.getElementById("prev").disabled = page === 0;
  document.getElementById("next").disabled = page >= pages - 1;
}

document.querySelectorAll("th").forEach(th => th.addEventListener("click", () => {
  const key = th.dataset.key;
  asc = sortKey === key ? !asc : true;
  sortKey = key;
  document.querySelectorAll("th").forEach(h => h.removeAttribute("aria-sort"));
  th.setAttribute("aria-sort", asc ? "ascending" : "descending");
  render();
}));

document.getElementById("filter").addEventListener("input", e => {
  query = e.target.value.toLowerCase(); page = 0; render();
});
document.getElementById("prev").onclick = () => { page--; render(); };
document.getElementById("next").onclick = () => { page++; render(); };
render();
</script>
```

`aria-sort` lets assistive technology announce the sort direction, and the arrows come from CSS so the markup stays clean. Each sort or filter change resets to page one, or the user could end up on an empty page.

Plan: one HTML file with no libraries. Amounts are stored as integer cents to avoid floating-point drift, data persists in localStorage, the bar chart is built from SVG elements, and export uses RFC 4180 CSV quoting.

